see "../src/harness.vox".
see "../src/runner.vox".
see "../src/sandbox.vox".

(Plan 323 T2 - the harness scratch sandbox. Proves the per-program
 scratch directory exists before the program runs and is gone after,
 no matter which of the three fates a fuzzed run ends in: a normal
 nonzero exit, death by signal, or a hang killed at the deadline. These
 are the normal cases in a fuzzer, not the exceptional ones - runner.vox
 and harness.vox already have to tell them apart for every generated
 program, so this test forces each one the same trusted way
 tests/020_harness.vox already does, with 'the scratch path' created
 and swept around the same supervise call.

 Confinement itself (bwrap, read-only-except-scratch, no network) is
 external - scripts/fuzz-confined.sh - and out of scope for this test,
 which exercises the directory lifecycle only.)

To 'prove sweep' with a number called seed and a text called label and a list called 'the arguments' and a number called 'the deadline in milliseconds'.
    a text called 'the scratch path' is 'scratch dir path' of seed.
    a number called 'the create result' is 'scratch dir create' of 'the scratch path'.
    a boolean called 'existed before' is false.
    If 'the scratch path' is available then,
        Set 'existed before' to true.
    a text called 'the verdict' is supervise of "/bin/sh" and 'the arguments' and 'the deadline in milliseconds'.
    a number called 'the sweep result' is 'scratch dir sweep' of 'the scratch path'.
    a boolean called 'exists after' is false.
    If 'the scratch path' is available then,
        Set 'exists after' to true.
    Print "{label}: verdict {'the verdict'} existed-before {'existed before'} exists-after {'exists after'}".

(1. Nonzero exit - the ordinary case, not a finding, and still swept.)
a list called 'the exit arguments' is ["-c", "exit 5"].
'prove sweep' of 501 and "nonzero exit" and 'the exit arguments' and 5000.

(2. Death by signal - the same kill-SEGV fixture tests/020_harness.vox
 uses, so this is a proven way to force a real WIFSIGNALED death.)
a list called 'the signal arguments' is ["-c", "kill -SEGV $$"].
'prove sweep' of 502 and "signal death" and 'the signal arguments' and 5000.

(3. Hang to the deadline - a busy loop of shell builtins (no sleep
 binary in the Nix sandbox, matching tests/020_harness.vox), killed by
 the harness itself at a short deadline. 700ms rather than
 020_harness.vox's 300ms - enough slack that a busy dev machine doesn't
 turn a real kill into a false failure, while still being short enough
 that this test stays fast.)
a list called 'the hang arguments' is ["-c", "while :; do :; done"].
'prove sweep' of 503 and "hang to deadline" and 'the hang arguments' and 700.

(4. The path handoff itself, end to end, through 'run program in
 scratch' rather than a hand-assembled argument list: a real compiled
 Vox program reads arguments's first, writes a file inside it, and the
 directory - file included - is gone once the run completes.)
a number called 'fixture compile result' is 'compile vox' of "." and "tests/fixtures/scratch_writer.vox" and "./vf_scratch_writer_bin" and "./vf_cerr".
a text called 'writer verdict' is 'run program in scratch' of "./vf_scratch_writer_bin" and 504 and 5000.
a text called 'writer scratch path' is 'scratch dir path' of 504.
a boolean called 'writer path exists after' is false.
If 'writer scratch path' is available then,
    Set 'writer path exists after' to true.
Print "argv handoff: verdict {'writer verdict'} exists-after {'writer path exists after'}".

(Each scenario above sweeps its own per-program subdirectory but leaves
 the shared scratch root behind by design - see sandbox.vox. Tidy the
 now-empty root so test.sh's own './vf_*' cleanup (which does not
 recurse into directories) has nothing left to complain about.)
a number called 'root cleanup' is 'run shell' of "rmdir {'the scratch root'} 2>/dev/null; true" and 2000.
